### 6. REAL REMAINING PRODUCT GAP
The unresolved product-level defect is narrow:

Current bulk stock upload resolves an item row from barcode, but the payload path historically retained only `item_code`. The Production adjustment contract is item-identity based, therefore the target must preserve `mappedItem.id` as `item_id` and pass that canonical identity into `bulk-stock-adjustment`.

Required surgical change:
```text
if(mappedItem){
  _uploadFileData[f].item_code=mappedItem.item_code;
  _uploadFileData[f].item_id=mappedItem.id;
}
```

and:
```text
items.push({
  item_id:_uploadFileData[u].item_id||null,
  item_code:_uploadFileData[u].item_code||_uploadFileData[u].barcode,
  qty:_uploadFileData[u].qty
});
```

No other target rewrite is justified by the evidence.

### 7. WHY THIS MATTERS ARCHITECTURALLY
The defect is not merely field completeness. It breaks the invariant that the UI must hand canonical item identity to the stock posting layer.

The same architectural principle appears in mature ERP systems. SAP documents a goods-movement/document principle in which each stock-changing event is represented by a movement document, with the movement updating stock and, when relevant, accounting in a traceable chain. citeturn124043search0turn124043search1

Microsoft Dynamics 365 Business Central similarly treats inventory increases/decreases as ledger-posting operations and explicitly distinguishes inventory changes from transfers between locations. citeturn124043search15

Odoo's current multi-company model also establishes explicit company context for records and restricts company-linked records to the current entity, while shared records are intentionally modeled as shared rather than relying on UI filtering. citeturn124043search4

Conclusion: RAWAEA's direction toward a canonical stock engine plus explicit tenant authority is architecturally sound and aligns with established ERP patterns.

